  "plot_summary": "When Reed snaps at the group for not paying attention to Dr. Doom's time machine, thus allowing a baby dinosaur to suddenly appear out of it, they all decide it's time to elect a new leader!\n\nThe next couple of pages show someone bailing criminals out of jail - three are specially hand-picked by the now-revealed Dr. Doom to help defeat the FF!\n\nAgain, a couple more pages of the other 3 FF members, trying to decide who should be the new leader, and of <i>course</i> Ben and Johnny start fighting over it (consuming pages) before Sue attempts to come between them.&nbsp; Finally, Reed shows up and shakes his head - only he has the maturity it seems to lead this cast of characters, and he orders them to clean up the mess while he focuses on locating Doom, whom he knows is still out there!&nbsp; (They last saw him plummeting to the ground at the end of issue #17.)\n\nBut Doom is busy giving extra powers to each of the three he freed from jail, one of which is flame-proof ability, and he tempts Johnny to come forward by offering to give a one million dollar car to the Human Torch!&nbsp; Like an idiot, he completely falls for it and is captured!\n\nMeanwhile, Ben is angered by a nasty letter from the Yancy Street Gang, and he storms over to clobber them, but one of the other three of Doom's gang is waiting, and his strength has been multiplied many times over now.&nbsp; But as they tangle, he shoots Ben with a cosmic ray beam that transforms him back to Ben Grimm, and he too is captured!\n\nAt the same time, Reed pretends to attack Sue to test her ability to defend herself, but she's still mad at him and heads home where flunkie #3 is waiting to give her flowers laced with a sleeping gas, but Sue senses the treachery and turns invisible, but the man, now enhanced with super hearing can tell where she is anyway.&nbsp; He sprays her with ether mist, and she finally succumbs.\n\nAll that's left is Mr. Fantastic, who Doom will tackle personally!&nbsp; Reed responds to an FF flare, thinking he sees Ben calling to him below, but it's actually a Thing robot, and soon he's trapped in a plexiglass case, the prisoner of Dr. Doom!&nbsp; Doom has his prisoners now, and he disposes of the three flunkies by transporting them to another dimension \"until your master has need for you again!\"\n\nBut Doom does not know about Sue's new power to cast invisible force fields, and she uses it to help Ben escape, who then smashes everyone else out.&nbsp; They battle, but to no avail, as Doom tries to maneuver them toward a \"solar wave\" which moves toward them and will send them out into space!&nbsp; But Sue extends her force shield around Doom, ensuring that if they die, so will he!&nbsp; He has no choice but to try and disable the device!&nbsp; Ben grabs him then and hurls him into space.&nbsp; \"HELLLLLP...!\"&nbsp; there he goes, floating into space!&nbsp; They barely have time themselves to crawl through the hole Doom came into the room before it's completely taken by space.\n\nBen is so grateful Reed knew what a solar wave was that he says Reed can remain the leader!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=System Error:]\n[HANG] You've reached your annual Doctor Doom quota.\n[HANG] Please try one of the following alternatives:\n[HANG2] * Mad Thinker\n[HANG2] * Puppet Master\n[HANG2] * Mole Man\n[HANG2] * Sub-Mariner\n\n<b>Marvel's response?</b>  [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=\"No.\"]\n\nIf <i>Fantastic Four</i> had modern software licensing rules, Doctor Doom would have been [SPAN: severity=error; text=locked out] of this book several issues ago.&nbsp; Instead, Marvel looked at its villain roster, shrugged, and concluded:\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=<i>Well</i>... one more couldn't hurt!]\n\nIt's as if Marvel owns twenty action figures, [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold; text=but keeps playing with the same one!]\n\nJames Smith said it best in the letters page of next month's <i>Amazing Spider-Man </i>10 (ASM #10, while referring to [LINK:1963:ASM_5|ASM #5|2026-12-01], which <i>also</i> featured Doom):&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=\"...has Dr. Doom got a financial interest in your comics?&nbsp; He turns up with monotonous regularity every month.\"]\n\nEven Marvel acknowledges the frequent appearances in their <b>Special Announcements Section</b> at the end of [SPAN: severity=error; text=this very issue:]\n\n[INDENT: both | 3rem] <i>It's a funny thing -- we get countless letters each week asking for more and more of the Doc.... But then, when we DO repeat some of our characters, we get almost as many letters from other readers saying they're sick of seeing them and telling us to think up NEW foes each issue.&nbsp; Sometimes we think the best way to edit a mag might be by just tossing a coin!</i>\n\nObviously that \"coin\" must have landed on [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Doom...<i>again</i>\"] this time around... [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=\"<i>funny thing</i>...\"]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n\nA solid enough effort.&nbsp; Always good to see Doom appear.&nbsp; He always has a plan going, and he always shows up again!\n\nIt seems Doom appears every few months in the FF, and I know I'm reading through these very quickly in comparison to what people were in the 1960s, so it appears he shows up every couple of months for me, which feels too often, but he's obviously very popular, so Lee and Kirby keep finding ways to bring him back, but this particular issue felt a bit too \"petty\" - he seems far more concerned with destroying the FF than any other matters.&nbsp; I know there is more to Doom which will be revealed later, but at this early point, he really feels like nothing more than a very powerful, very formidable super-villain.&nbsp; We'll see how this keeps developing in these pages, but there's nothing really that <i>genuinely</i> feels elevated about his character other than his obvious brilliance, cleverness and seemingly-unlimited resources.&nbsp; This downgrades my ultimate enjoyment of the story, but it's still a solid effort worthy of the mid-grade rating.&nbsp; Doom's treatment of his three flunkies is completely in character.\n\nThe wasted pages of the FF fighting amongst themselves was again nothing more than a way to fill pages.&nbsp; It happens in almost every single issue.&nbsp; That also prevented the book from getting a higher grade.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe book was...well...\"fine\" and nothing more.&nbsp; Good, but not notable.\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nThe letters page hints that Captain America might soon be joining the Avengers!&nbsp; There's also a letter only about the X-Men, which seemed out of place.",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}